There have been significant modifications made to federal immigration laws since the COVID-19 pandemic started in March 2020 in order to protect public health while allowing for the safe and legal entry of certain individuals from Asia into the United States. According to the US Department of Homeland Security, certain individuals from Asia are still allowed to enter the US if they meet certain criteria, such as if they:

- Have a valid, approved travel document;
- Are outside the United States when the public health emergency is declared;
- Have a close family relationship with a US citizen or lawful permanent resident;
- Are a refugee or asylee;
- Have a valid O, P, or Q visa;
- Are a participant in a designated Exchange Visitor Program;
- Are a national of one of the countries that qualifies for the Visa Waiver Program; or
- Are a lawful permanent resident who has been outside the United States for at least one year.
